  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nThrough Thursday: Local radars showed a few showers developing over \nthe southwest where there is just enough moisture to support them. \nIn this area heat index values were climbing into the lower 100s and \nwill top out near 105F. The convection will see a distinct diurnal \ntrend and dissipate shortly after sunset. Unfortunately the heat \nand humidity will increase over more of our CWA Thursday.  Latest \nRAP/water vapor analysis still showed an upper low situated northern \nGA and the corner of the Carolinas and a 595DM mid level ridge over \nthe Desert Southwest. The upper low will gradually weaken through \nThursday and along with a low level southerly return flow, low and \ndeep layer moisture will will increase. Thus humidity will be higher \nThursday while temperatures top out warmer than normal as well. \nAfternoon highs in the 94F to 97F range will lead to heat index \nvalues of 105F to 108F over our southwest and southern zones with \nsites in our delta peaking near 105F. Our northeast will see another \nday with heat indices well below 105F. /22/ \n\nLate week into next week (Friday through Tuesday): As troughing \ncontinues to lose influence across the area amid rising heights, low \nlevel southerly return flow and an uptick in moisture (low and deep \nlayer moisture) will favor less efficient mixing, warmth/moist \nadvection and increasing heat stress late week through the weekend. \n\nTemperatures will be seasonably warm (highs in the 94 F to 97 F \nrange and lows in the 72 F to 76 F range). The region will remain in \nmore of a large scale weakness in the flow but low level flow will \nhelp moderate in higher surface dewpoints into the weekend (72 F to \n76 F) and increasing heat stress concerns. Graphical depictions of \nthis forecast period remain unchanged, but forecast trends suggest \nan expansion of the \"Elevated\" risk area to include more if not all \nof the area may be required heading into next week. \n\nLow level moist advection and increased deep moisture aloft \ncontinues to increase into the weekend (PWs in the 1.5 to 2 inch \nrange by the weekend into early next week). Mostly afternoon and \nearly evening, diurnally driven rain chances spreads northward in\ntandem with the increasing humidity into next week. Coverage will\nbe isolated to at times scattered coverage (20 to 45 percent \nrange) to a little more numerous in the Pine Belt early next week \nfrom deep moisture (PWs in excess of 2.0 inches) and potentially \nsea breeze influence. This pattern will not favor any noteworthy \nstorm threat, but a few stronger, gusty storms with locally heavy \nrain cannot be ruled out. /86/DC/",
